{"found":true,"status":"running","message":null,"revision":1,"steps":[{"key":"prepare","label":"Reading your topic and brand voice","state":"done"},{"key":"write","label":"Writing your post","state":"active"},{"key":"check","label":"Checking facts, tone and quality","state":"pending"},{"key":"revise","label":"Revising what the checks flagged","state":"pending"},{"key":"save","label":"Saving to My Content","state":"pending"}],"sections":[{"key":"body","label":"","text":"Pakistan's folk traditions span centuries and dozens of regional communities — and Lok Virsa Museum in Islamabad brings them together in one place.

Established in 1974, Lok Virsa Museum is devoted to the folk and traditional heritage of Pakistan. Its collections move across textiles, costumes, jewellery, woodwork, ceramics, and musical instruments, each one representing a specific community or region. For families, that breadth matters: there is genuine variety here, and the objects themselves carry the stories of the people who made and used them. The museum sits in Islamabad's Shakarparian area and is associated with the National Institute of Folk and Traditional Heritage.

